+\paragraph{Related work on prescribed orders and norms.}
+The existence of elements of prescribed multiplicative order under
+additive relations is a classical theme. The line and translate
+properties for $r$-primitive elements were studied by Cohen and
+Kapetanakis~\cite{CK21}. Their Theorem~1.3 fixes $r$ and the
+extension degree; Remark~4.1 also observes that sufficiently slow
+growth of $r$ can be allowed. Thus varying $r$ alone does not
+establish a distinction from that work.
+Cohen, Kapetanakis and Reis~\cite{CKR22} developed an $(r,n)$-freeness
+indicator and sieve, including applications to primitive points on
+curves. Arithmetic progressions of $r$-primitive elements are treated
+in~\cite{SSL24}, while additional norm constraints are studied in
+\cite{CSST25,CSh25}. These papers provide important predecessors for
+the use of multiplicative characters and prescribed-order sieves.
+Our quartic problem requires exact order $(t+1)(t^2+1)$ and
+simultaneously requires the translate by $1$ to lie in the same
+norm-one subgroup. These constraints require a comparison of
+quantitative hypotheses, as well as of prescribed orders. For
+example, the sufficient condition in \cite[Theorem~1]{CSh25}
+contains $q^{n/2-m}$ on its left-hand side; for base field size
+$q=t$, extension degree $n=4$ and two terms $m=2$, this is $1$,
+whereas its right-hand side is at least $2$. That particular
+criterion therefore gives no positivity in our parameters.
+The associated \emph{ordinary} cyclotomic intersection count belongs
+to the theory studied, in particular, by Kudo and Nogata~\cite{KN26};
+our additional exact-order requirement is separate from this count.
+We use the classical Lang--Kummer and conductor methods rather than
+claiming a new general character-sum theorem. The specific norm-fiber
+construction and its application to the critical realization problem
+are the contributions developed below. No claim of priority is made
+solely on the basis of the literature compared here.
+
